WHY "The email deliverability test is not available right now"
----------------------------------------------------------------
The tool needs its own mailbox to receive the test emails, and none is configured yet.
It turns on automatically once ALL FOUR of these have a value:
  EMAIL_TEST_ADDRESS, EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_HOST, EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_USERNAME, EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_PASSWORD
Until then the public page hides the section (hard refresh if you still see it)
and the dashboard page shows "The test mailbox is not configured on this server."


STEP 1 — Create the test mailbox (one time)
--------------------------------------------
Use a dedicated mailbox that does nothing else, e.g. mailtest@veravalonline.com.
(By default, test emails are deleted from it after they are read.)
It must support plus-addressing (mailtest+anything@...). Google Workspace and Microsoft 365 both do.

Google Workspace:
  - Create the user, turn on 2-Step Verification, then create an App Password
    (Google Account > Security > App passwords).
  - IMAP is on by default for Workspace (Gmail settings > Forwarding and POP/IMAP > Enable IMAP).
  - Host: imap.gmail.com   Trusted authserv id: mx.google.com

Microsoft 365:
  - Create the user, enable IMAP for it (Admin center > Users > Mail > Manage email apps > IMAP),
    and allow basic/app-password auth for IMAP if your tenant blocks it.
  - Microsoft 365 has plus-addressing on by default for new tenants
    (check: Exchange admin > Settings > Mail flow > "Turn on plus addressing").
  - Host: outlook.office365.com   Trusted authserv id: leave EMPTY (Microsoft doesn't send one)

Test the login first with any mail app (Thunderbird etc.) before putting it in VendorPulse.


STEP 2 — The env values
------------------------
# --- Send-a-test-email tool (required to enable it) ---
EMAIL_TEST_ADDRESS=mailtest@veravalonline.com
EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_HOST=imap.gmail.com
EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_USERNAME=mailtest@veravalonline.com
EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_PASSWORD=<app password>
EMAIL_TEST_TRUSTED_AUTHSERV_ID=mx.google.com

# --- Optional (these are the defaults; only add if you want to change them) ---
EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_PORT=993
EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_ENCRYPTION=ssl
EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_FOLDER=INBOX
EMAIL_TEST_DELETE_AFTER_READ=true
EMAIL_TEST_EXPIRES_MINUTES=30          # how long a test address stays valid
EMAIL_TEST_INBOX_CHECK_INTERVAL=5      # seconds between inbox checks while someone waits
EMAIL_TEST_RETENTION_DAYS=7            # old test results are deleted after this

# --- Email security checks on the DNS Check (optional) ---
EMAIL_SECURITY_SPAMHAUS_DQS_KEY=       # free key from spamhaus.com (Data Query Service).
                                       # Without it, the Spamhaus blacklists show as "unverified" because
                                       # Spamhaus refuses queries from public/Docker DNS resolvers.
EMAIL_SECURITY_BLACKLIST_ENABLED=true  # set false to skip all blacklist lookups
EMAIL_SECURITY_BLACKLIST_MAX_IPS=2     # how many mail-server IPs to check per domain
EMAIL_SECURITY_DKIM_SELECTORS=         # comma list; replaces the built-in list of 34 common selectors
EMAIL_SECURITY_MAX_MX_HOSTS=5
EMAIL_SECURITY_HTTP_TIMEOUT=5          # seconds, for MTA-STS/BIMI fetches


STEP 3a — Production
---------------------
The containers read /var/www/vendorpulse/.env.docker (mounted as the app's .env).
Add the values to BOTH .env and .env.docker (keep them in sync, same as NEXTAUTH_SECRET).

cd /var/www/vendorpulse
nano .env          # add the lines from Step 2
nano .env.docker   # add the same lines
bash vpproduction.sh deploy     # runs migrate + rebuilds the config cache
                                # (the email test needs the new migration anyway)

Verify (should print bool(true)):
docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.bind.yml exec app \
  php artisan tinker --execute='var_dump(app(App\Services\Vapt\Email\EmailDeliverabilityTestService::class)->isAvailable());'

Important: if you only edit .env.docker without deploy, also run
  docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.bind.yml exec app php artisan optimize
and restart app + scheduler, because production caches config.


STEP 3b — Local (WSL)
----------------------
Locally the .env file is NOT copied into the container; the app only gets the variables
listed under "environment:" in docker-compose.yml. So adding lines to .env alone does nothing.

Create docker-compose.override.yml in the project root (docker compose loads it automatically
on local; production uses explicit -f files so it is ignored there — do not commit it):

services:
  app:
    environment: &email_env
      EMAIL_TEST_ADDRESS: ${EMAIL_TEST_ADDRESS:-}
      EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_HOST: ${EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_HOST:-}
      EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_USERNAME: ${EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_USERNAME:-}
      EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_PASSWORD: ${EMAIL_TEST_IMAP_PASSWORD:-}
      EMAIL_TEST_TRUSTED_AUTHSERV_ID: ${EMAIL_TEST_TRUSTED_AUTHSERV_ID:-}
      EMAIL_SECURITY_SPAMHAUS_DQS_KEY: ${EMAIL_SECURITY_SPAMHAUS_DQS_KEY:-}
  scheduler:
    environment: *email_env

Then put the values in the local .env and run:
docker compose build scheduler horizon     # these images are still on old code (no email-test schedule yet)
docker compose up -d app scheduler horizon

Note: a password containing "$" must be written as "$$" in .env for docker compose.


STEP 4 — Try it
----------------
1. Open http://127.0.0.1:8000/ (or the live site) > "Email deliverability test" > Get test address.
2. Send an email to that address from Outlook/Gmail/your website.
3. Result should appear within ~5–60 seconds (the page checks every 5 s; the scheduler also checks every minute).
If it never arrives: check the mailbox itself received it, then
  docker compose logs --tail=50 scheduler   (look for IMAP login errors).


